{"name":"Federal Blue","hex":"#43628b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"federal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to a league or treaty; derived from an agreement or covenant between parties, especially between nations.","Pertaining to the national government level in a federal nation, as opposed to state, provincial, county, city, or town."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A federal agent or (chiefly law-enforcement) official.","A law-enforcement official of the FBI."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/federal"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shades of blue","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color blue may differ in hue, chroma, or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a blue or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Shades_of_Blue.svg/330px-Shades_of_Blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_blue"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/1280px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg/500px-A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg/1280px-A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_